WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:05.440
Your most compelling sounds already exist.

2
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:09.520
They are buried in the reverb tales of
tracks you

3
00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:14.080
forgot, hidden deep beneath the surface of
the work you've

4
00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:18.960
already finished. These are the
subharmonics of a pad you

5
00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:22.880
used once, or the specific room tone of a
recording

6
00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:26.160
made in a different house, a different
year.

7
00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:30.080
To a listener, these fragments are
invisible.

8
00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:35.840
But as isolated artifacts, they hold
immense creative value, acting

9
00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:40.720
as unique sonic pigments derived from your
own history rather

10
00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:43.600
than a generic sample library.

11
00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:47.600
Every producer knows the specific
frustration of reaching for a

12
00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:51.600
texture that feels familiar but doesn't
exist in their current

13
00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:56.080
toolkit. It is a sound they remember
making years ago,

14
00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:58.480
trapped in an old project.

15
00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:04.040
Manual extraction is a slow, multi-step
process, locating old hard

16
00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:09.640
drives, booting up obsolete session files,
and soloing individual tracks

17
00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:13.080
just to bounce a single grain of sound.

18
00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:16.760
This mechanical delay destroys the
creative flow state.

19
00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:19.480
By the time the sound is retrieved, the
impulse that

20
00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:21.320
wanted it has often vanished.

21
00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:24.440
The barrier to reusing our own sonic
history is the

22
00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:27.080
sheer weight of the excavation itself.

23
00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:30.840
Afterglow engine functions as an automated
archaeology tool.

24
00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:33.720
It is designed to mine finished audio
files for the

25
00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:35.640
textures buried inside them.

26
00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:38.600
You feed the software an entire completed
album.

27
00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:41.800
It treats the finished waveforms as
physical terrain to be

28
00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:43.320
surveyed and processed.

29
00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:47.960
The engine identifies and separates hidden
elements into reusable categories,

30
00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:52.120
extracting padbeds, forming granular
clouds, and layers of hiss or

31
00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:56.160
air. This automation bridges the gap
between a creator's memory

32
00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:59.920
and instant audio retrieval, removing the
time sink that usually

33
00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:01.840
kills the creative spark.

34
00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:05.120
Instant analysis of an entire album is
made possible through

35
00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:10.800
a mathematical mechanism called short-time
Fourier transform, or STFT caching.

36
00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:15.600
Standard tools are slow, constantly
recalculating frequency data.

37
00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:20.640
SFT caching replaces this with a
streamed-lined pipeline retrieving pre-calculated

38
00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:24.160
data. Because it calculates once, the
magnitude of the difference

39
00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:28.720
is stark, 100,000 times speed up over
naive analysis.

40
00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:31.520
Speed alone, however, doesn't protect the
audio.

41
00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:35.840
To ensure this high-velocity extraction
doesn't degrade the original fidelity,

42
00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:40.560
the software runs 149 automated tests and
monitors for performance

43
00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:45.840
regressions. These extracted ghosts are
delivered as 44.1 kHz WAV

44
00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:49.360
files, optimized for immediate use in
hardware samplers.

45
00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:52.600
While the caching architecture enables the
workflow, it is this

46
00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:56.680
rigorous testing suite that safeguards the
artifacts themselves, ensuring the

47
00:02:56.680 --> 00:02:59.960
engine preserves the very textures it's
designed to find.

48
00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:03.000
This level of speed and excavation alters
how a musician

49
00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:04.840
can view a finished track.

50
00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:07.400
We usually view a completed album as the
end point

51
00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:09.000
of a creative cycle.

52
00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:11.880
But when those files are treated as a raw
sample

53
00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:15.560
library, every finished track becomes a
starting point for the

54
00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:19.800
next one. This perspective transforms a
past catalog into an

55
00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:21.960
active, living ecosystem.

56
00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:24.760
It is no longer a dead archive, but a
source

57
00:03:24.760 --> 00:03:27.480
of raw material ready for harvest.

58
00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:30.040
These pigments are inherently personal.

59
00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:33.400
They carry the DNA of your past recording
environments and

60
00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:36.440
the specific sonic choices you made years
ago.

61
00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:40.280
By making these sounds instantly
accessible, the engine allows you

62
00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:43.960
to continuously collaborate with older
versions of yourself.

63
00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:46.960
You no longer have to look outward for new
textures.

64
00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:50.080
The future of your sound is already
waiting within the

65
00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:51.920
ghosts of your past.

